/// A parameter's type may be followed by '...', collecting the rest.
///
/// '...' is not a token today -- Pascal's range operator was never adopted, so
/// neither '..' nor '...' scans -- and the parameter list fails with
/// 'Expect '')'' after parameters.'
///
/// ⚠️ The spelling is safe here in a way it is not in every language, because a
/// TYPE IS ALWAYS A BARE IDENTIFIER: 'var C : Shapes.Circle' is rejected while
/// 'var C : Circle' is accepted, so a dot never follows a type name anywhere in
/// the grammar and 'String...' can collide with nothing.
///
/// Inside the function the parameter is a List of T, which is why Length and
/// indexing are asserted here rather than anything new.  Supplying no arguments
/// gives a list of length zero and never nil -- the third case exists to pin
/// that, since an empty variadic is where an implementation is most likely to
/// hand back nothing at all.
///
/// Fails with: Expect ')' after parameters.  (file rejected -- no test ran)
///
function Join (Sep : String, Parts : String...) : String;
var
    Result : String := '';

begin
    for var I := 0; I < Parts.Length; I := I + 1 do
    begin
        if I > 0 then Result := Result + Sep;
        Result := Result + Parts[I];
    end

    Exit Result;
end

test 'A variadic parameter collects the remaining arguments';
begin
    AssertEqual (Str ('a-b-c'), Join (Str ('-'), Str ('a'), Str ('b'), Str ('c')));
    AssertEqual (Str ('a'),     Join (Str ('-'), Str ('a')));
    AssertEqual (Str (''),      Join (Str ('-')));
end
